const e=[{id:"pf-lesson-reason",moduleId:"m13-legal-reasoning",title:"Building an argument, one step at a time",minutes:20,summary:"Turn a story into a question, break the rule into parts, put one fact against each part, and then say what follows.",plants:["pf-reason-01","pf-reason-02","pf-reason-03","pf-reason-04","pf-reason-05","pf-reason-06"],prepares:["pf-prob-reason"],reading:[{bookId:"williams-learning-the-law",where:"on solving legal problems — read it after you have tried one, not before"},{bookId:"schauer-thinking-like-a-lawyer",where:"on analogy and precedent, once the steps here feel ordinary"}],source:"Standard problem-question technique and standard common law reasoning by analogy. The facts are invented for this lesson.",lastVerified:"2026-09-07",verify:"Nothing here states a rule of Malaysian law and nothing needs a citator. The rule used in the worked example is a shape, not an authority. Before using it on real facts, find the governing statute or the actual case law on the point.",plate:"ladder-argument",plateCaption:"An argument built one rung at a time. Skipping a rung is what makes an answer feel confident and score badly.",readMinutes:7,sections:[{id:"sec-pf-lesson-reason-0",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"A story is not yet a legal question, and turning one into the other is the first move.",h:"A story is not yet a question",body:[{t:"p",text:"Rosli parks his motorbike outside a kedai runcit in Klang. The owner moves it so a delivery lorry can get to the door, and drops it. The mirror snaps off."},{t:"p",text:"That is a story. Nothing in it tells you what there is to argue about. The first move in legal reasoning is turning a story into a question that a rule can answer."},{t:"p",text:"Beginners skip this step and start reciting rules. The rules then have nothing to bite on, and the answer reads like a list of facts about the law."}]},{id:"sec-pf-lesson-reason-1",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"An issue is a question the facts leave genuinely open; if only one answer is possible there is no issue.",h:"Spotting the issue",body:[{t:"p",text:"An issue is a question the facts leave genuinely open. Both answers are arguable, and it is stated in legal words rather than in feelings."},{t:"list",items:["Too vague: was the shop owner in the wrong.","Better: did the shop owner owe Rosli a duty to take care while moving the bike.","Better still: and if he did, was dropping it a failure to take that care."]},{t:"p",text:"Notice the shape of the good versions. Each names a legal idea. Each can be answered yes or no. Each has a decent argument on both sides."},{t:"rule",text:"An issue is a legal question the facts leave open. If only one answer is possible, it is not the issue.",source:"Standard problem-question technique"},{t:"caution",text:"Most sets of facts hide two or three issues rather than one. Find them all before you argue any of them. Otherwise you spend your best paragraph on the easy one and run out of room."}]},{id:"sec-pf-lesson-reason-2",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"Break a rule into its elements and test each one, because an element you never mention is one you never proved.",h:"Break the rule into parts",body:[{t:"p",text:"A legal rule is almost never one thing. It is a short list of conditions, and normally all of them must be met."},{t:"example",text:"Take a rule in this shape: a person who takes charge of another's property must take reasonable care of it, and answers for loss caused by failing to do so. That is four questions. Did he take charge? Was it another's property? Did he fall below reasonable care? Did that failure cause the loss?"},{t:"p",text:"Write the parts out, one per line, before you argue anything. Then take them one at a time. This is dull, and it is most of why a trained answer looks organised and an untrained one does not."},{t:"rule",text:"Split every rule into its elements and test each element against a fact. An element you never mention is an element you never proved.",source:"Standard problem-question technique"}]},{id:"sec-pf-lesson-reason-3",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"Application means putting one specific fact next to one specific element — not retelling the story.",h:"One fact per element",body:[{t:"p",text:"Application means putting a specific fact next to a specific element. It is not retelling the story, and it is not restating the rule in longer words."},{t:"example",text:"Weak: the shop owner was careless, so he must pay. Strong: he lifted a loaded bike on his own, on a wet five-foot way, while two men stood beside him doing nothing. That is what falling below reasonable care looks like on these facts."},{t:"caution",text:"Here is a quick test. If a sentence in your answer would still be true after you changed the facts, it is not application. It is padding, and it earns nothing."}]},{id:"sec-pf-lesson-reason-4",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"Put the other side's best version, then decide; leaving it hanging throws away the answer.",h:"Argue the other side, then decide",body:[{t:"p",text:"Every real issue has a second side. Write it out. A one-sided answer looks like you never noticed the difficulty."},{t:"example",text:"For the shop owner: he moved the bike to let a delivery through, Rosli had left it against the door, and nobody asked him to become its keeper. That account fits the same facts. Two accounts fitting the same facts is what makes something an issue."},{t:"rule",text:"Put the strongest version of the other side, then say which side is stronger and why. Do not leave the question hanging.",source:"Standard problem-question technique"},{t:"p",text:"A conclusion may be tentative. It may not be absent. On balance, and for these reasons, is a proper ending to an answer."}]},{id:"sec-pf-lesson-reason-5",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"An earlier case is close only on the facts its result actually depended on.",h:"Using an earlier case",body:[{t:"p",text:"Often the strongest thing you have is a decided case with facts like yours. Using it well means saying why it is like yours."},{t:"p",text:"Ask first which facts the earlier decision actually turned on. If those facts are here too, the case helps you. If one of them is missing, the other side will say so, and lawyers call that distinguishing the case."},{t:"rule",text:"A case is close only on the facts its result depended on. A difference matters only if it goes to the reason for the earlier decision.",source:"Standard common law reasoning"},{t:"caution",text:"Any two cases differ in a hundred ways. Different people, a different town, a different year. Pointing at a difference is not yet an argument. You have to say why that difference should change the answer."},{t:"diagram",kind:"branch",title:"When an earlier case is actually close",caption:"Any two cases differ in a hundred ways. Different people, a different town, a different year. Pointing at a difference is not yet an argument.",alt:"A question — are the facts the earlier decision turned on present here too? — branching two ways. Yes: the case helps you, and you can say why it is like yours. No, one of them is missing: the other side has something to argue, and you must say why the difference goes to the reason for the earlier decision.",question:"Are the facts that decision turned on here too?",branches:[{cond:"yes",label:"The case helps you",note:"say why it is like yours"},{cond:"one is missing",label:"The other side has an argument",note:"and you must meet it"}]}]},{id:"sec-pf-lesson-reason-6",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"Write the motorbike problem in five visible steps, so you can see your own structure.",h:"What to do with this",body:[{t:"p",text:"Six cards, then a problem about a dropped motorbike. Write it in the five steps above, in order, with the steps visible on the page. Being able to see your own structure is half the point."},{t:"steps",title:"The five steps, in order, with the steps visible on the page",items:[{h:"Spot the issue.",text:"Turn the story into a legal question the facts leave open. Most sets of facts hide two or three, so find them all before you argue any of them."},{h:"Break the rule into parts.",text:"A rule is a short list of conditions. Write them one per line before you argue anything. An element you never mention is an element you never proved."},{h:"One fact per element.",text:"Put a specific fact next to a specific element. If a sentence would still be true after you changed the facts, it is padding and it earns nothing."},{h:"Argue the other side.",text:"Put the strongest version of it, then say which side is stronger and why. A conclusion may be tentative. It may not be absent."},{h:"Use an earlier case, carefully.",text:"A case is close only on the facts its result depended on. A difference matters only if it goes to the reason for that decision."}]}]}],quiz:[{id:"pf-q-reason-1",q:"What makes a question an issue?",options:["It is the first question you thought of","It is about a rule you know well","The facts leave it open and both answers are arguable","The parties disagree about what happened"],answer:2,why:"If only one answer is possible, it is not the issue. Find the ones that are actually open.",source:"Standard problem-question technique"},{id:"pf-q-reason-2",q:"What is the first thing to do with a rule before you apply it?",options:["Split it into elements and list them","Find a case about it","Write it out in full","Decide who ought to win"],answer:0,why:"An element you never mention is an element you never proved.",source:"Standard problem-question technique; a rule is applied element by element"},{id:"pf-q-reason-3",q:"Which of these is application?",options:["He was careless, so he must pay","The law requires reasonable care","He lifted a loaded bike alone on a wet walkway with help standing beside him","The bike was damaged"],answer:2,why:"Application ties one fact to one element. The others restate the rule or the story.",source:"Standard problem-question technique; the application band of the rubric"},{id:"pf-q-reason-4",q:"Why write out the other side's best argument?",options:["It fills space","It lets you avoid taking a position","A real issue has two arguable sides and you have to show you saw both","Longer answers score better"],answer:2,why:"You set out the difficulty and then decide. Setting it out is not the same as ducking it.",source:"Standard problem-question technique"},{id:"pf-q-reason-5",q:"Before using an earlier case, what must you identify?",options:["The name of the judge","How long the judgment is","Whether it is recent","The facts the earlier decision actually turned on"],answer:3,why:"Only those facts make it close to yours, and only a difference in them is worth arguing.",source:"Standard common law method on ratio decidendi"}]},{id:"l-analogy",moduleId:"m13-legal-reasoning",title:"Analogy, distinguishing, and the honest use of precedent",minutes:25,summary:"Distinguishing is an argument about which facts mattered, not a way of dodging an awkward case. Doing it badly is the most visible weakness in a legal answer.",plants:["mm-analogy-01","mm-analogy-02","mm-analogy-03","mm-analogy-04"],prepares:["pa-distinguish-01"],reading:[{bookId:"williams-learning-the-law",where:"the chapters on precedent and on legal reasoning"}],source:"Standard common law method on precedent, distinguishing and persuasive authority; the codification point follows from the Contracts Act 1950 occupying the field.",lastVerified:"2026-09-07",verify:"Check the current practice of the Federal Court on departing from its own previous decisions before asserting anything about how freely it may do so — the position has been stated in several judgments and is not identical in all of them.",plate:"reasoning",plateCaption:"Dividers stepping between two cases. Distinguishing is a measurement of whether the earlier reasoning reaches these facts.",readMinutes:5,sections:[{id:"sec-l-analogy-0",readMinutes:2,keypoint:"Distinguishing is a claim about materiality, not a way of escaping a case you dislike.",h:"Almost no case is exactly like yours",body:[{t:"p",text:"You find a case that looks like yours. It is not identical, because nothing ever is. Does it help you, or does the other side get to use it?"},{t:"p",text:"The work is arguing that the differences do not matter, or that they do. Both arguments have the same shape: both are claims about which facts the earlier decision actually turned on."},{t:"p",text:"Showing that a binding case turned on a fact your case lacks has a name. Lawyers call it distinguishing."},{t:"predict",prompt:"The binding case was about a rented shophouse and yours is about a rented flat. Nothing in the earlier reasoning turns on the kind of building. Before you read on — have you distinguished it?",options:["No, because the difference has to be one the earlier reasoning leaned on","Yes, because the facts are plainly different","Only if the other side cannot find a closer case"],answer:0,reveal:"Pointing at a difference is not distinguishing. Every case differs from every other in a hundred ways. If any difference would do, no decision would ever bind anybody. The first answer is the honest first instinct, and it is what weak answers do at length. The difference has to be one the earlier court's reasoning actually leaned on."},{t:"rule",text:"To distinguish a binding case, identify a material difference between its facts and yours — a difference such that the reasoning of the earlier decision does not reach your facts. A difference that the earlier reasoning does not depend on is not a distinction.",source:"Standard common law method; follows from the definition of ratio decidendi"},{t:"p",text:"Put simply: it is not enough that the two cases differ. The difference has to be one the earlier court's reasoning actually leaned on."},{t:"caution",text:"'The earlier case was about a lorry and ours is about a van' is not a distinction, unless something in the reasoning turned on the size of the vehicle. Pointing at a difference is not distinguishing. You have to show that the difference does work."}]},{id:"sec-l-analogy-1",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"Three moves in descending order of legitimacy, and a student answer is almost always making the first.",anchor:"fig-l-analogy-0",h:"Three moves, strongest first",body:[{t:"list",items:["**Distinguish.** The rule of the earlier case, stated properly, does not stretch to these facts. Legitimate, and open to any court.","**Confine it to its facts.** The earlier decision is read as narrowly as its result allows. Legitimate but weaker, and a court doing it is usually signalling that it is uncomfortable.","**Depart from it, or overrule it.** Open only to a court with the power to do it. The Federal Court may depart from its own earlier decisions, but does so sparingly and with reasons."]},{t:"p",text:"Almost always the move you want is the first. Saying that a Federal Court decision is simply wrong is not an argument a High Court can make. Writing as though it were shows that the hierarchy has not landed."},{t:"diagram",kind:"stack",title:"Three moves, in descending order of legitimacy",caption:"A student answer should almost always be doing the first. Asserting that a Federal Court decision is wrong is not an argument available to the High Court.",alt:"Three moves, most available first. Distinguish: available to any court, and a claim about materiality. Confine the case to its facts: legitimate but weaker, and usually a signal of discomfort. Depart from it or overrule it: only a court with the power, and sparingly.",layers:[{label:"Distinguish",note:"available to any court; a claim about materiality"},{label:"Confine to its facts",note:"legitimate but weaker — usually a signal of discomfort"},{label:"Depart or overrule",note:"only a court with the power, and sparingly"}]}]},{id:"sec-l-analogy-2",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"Foreign decisions persuade rather than bind, and weigh least where a Malaysian statute occupies the field.",h:"Persuasive authority, and how to weigh it",body:[{t:"p",text:"Decisions of courts in other common law countries do not bind a Malaysian court. They are persuasive authority: a court may follow them but need not."},{t:"p",text:"Their weight depends on three things. How similar the statutory scheme is, how senior the court was, and how well the reasoning fits conditions here."},{t:"caution",text:"The trap in Malaysian law is the codified subject, where Parliament has written the rules into an Act. Contract here is governed by the Contracts Act 1950, not by the English common law of contract. An English case helps only where the Act's words raise the same question. An English authority cited against the words of a Malaysian section is worse than no authority at all."}]},{id:"sec-l-analogy-3",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"Set the earlier facts beside yours and say what differs and why it matters — three sentences beat a paragraph.",h:"Write it so a reader can check you",body:[{t:"p",text:"Set out the earlier case's material facts. State its rule in your own words. Put your facts beside it, and say what differs and why the difference matters."},{t:"p",text:"Three sentences done properly beat a paragraph of assertion."},{t:"example",text:"*The authority in the drainage case had taken on the job of maintenance and then abandoned it, and the loss was physical damage to goods. Here the authority never started the work, and the loss is lost trade. The first difference goes to whether a duty arose at all. The second was a point the earlier court expressly left open. That case does not decide ours.*"},{t:"p",text:"That is what the application band of a marking rubric is looking for. It is also why an answer that recites two cases and asserts a conclusion scores far lower than it feels like it should."}]}],quiz:[{id:"qan1",q:"What makes a difference between two cases a genuine distinction?",options:["The facts are not identical","The parties are different","The earlier case is older","The earlier reasoning depends on the fact that differs"],answer:3,why:"Distinguishing is a claim about what mattered. If the earlier reasoning did not turn on it, pointing at it proves nothing.",source:"Standard common law method on distinguishing"},{id:"qan2",q:"A High Court judge thinks a Federal Court decision is wrong. What is the legitimate move?",options:["Distinguish it if the facts allow, and otherwise follow it","Decline to follow it","Overrule it","Treat it as persuasive only"],answer:0,why:"The hierarchy binds. Departing from Federal Court authority is not a move open to the High Court.",source:"Standard common law doctrine of stare decisis"},{id:"qan3",q:"Why are English contract cases used with particular care in Malaysia?",options:["English cases are never persuasive","Contract is codified in the Contracts Act 1950","The English courts use a different standard of proof","Contract law is a state matter"],answer:1,why:"The starting point is the words of the Act. An English authority helps only where the Act raises the same question.",source:"Contracts Act 1950 (Act 136); contract is codified"},{id:"qan4",q:"Which is the weakest of the three legitimate ways of handling an awkward precedent?",options:["Distinguishing it","Confining it to its facts","Following it","Applying it by analogy"],answer:1,why:"It is legitimate, but it usually signals discomfort rather than a reasoned limit.",source:"Standard common law method on precedent"},{id:"qan5",q:"What does the application band of a rubric reward?",options:["Putting the rule against these facts and showing what turns on the difference","Accurate recitation of the earlier cases","Naming the correct section","A firm conclusion"],answer:0,why:"Reciting is the rule band. Application is the work of moving from the rule to the facts in front of you.",source:"The rubric bands used by the problem questions in this app"}]},{id:"l-policy-in-judging",moduleId:"m13-legal-reasoning",title:"Policy arguments in judicial reasoning",minutes:30,readMinutes:6,summary:"Sometimes the rules and the cases run out, and a judge must ask what a ruling would do to everyone in a similar position. Malaysian courts say so openly in a few places, such as the third limb of the Caparo test, and do it quietly in many more.",source:"General jurisprudential material on policy reasoning in adjudication, illustrated with the third limb of the Caparo test for a duty of care.",verify:"Nothing here states a rule of Malaysian law and none of it needs a citator beyond the Caparo citation itself. Check the current Malaysian formulation of the duty of care test before relying on Caparo directly.",lastVerified:"2026-09-11",reading:[{bookId:"schauer-thinking-like-a-lawyer",where:"on consequences and policy in judicial reasoning"},{bookId:"bingham-rule-of-law",where:"on how far judges should openly rely on their own view of good policy"}],sections:[{id:"sec-l-policy-in-judging-0",readMinutes:2,keypoint:"Yusof had no contract with the bank at all, yet the bank wants him to answer for its loss.",h:"A question the rules do not answer",body:[{t:"p",text:"Encik Yusof is a licensed valuer in Johor Bahru. A bank asks him to value a house before approving a loan."},{t:"p",text:"He values the house at RM650,000. He was careless, and it is worth closer to RM400,000."},{t:"p",text:"The borrower stops paying, the bank sells the house, and the shortfall is RM250,000."},{t:"p",text:"The bank never hired Yusof. The borrower did. Nothing in his contract with the borrower answers whether he owes the bank anything at all."},{t:"rule",text:"Sometimes no existing rule or precedent decides a case squarely, and a court must reason its way to an answer instead of reading one off. A policy argument asks what a ruling would do to everyone in a similar position, not only to the parties in front of the court.",source:"Standard jurisprudential material on judicial reasoning"}]},{id:"sec-l-policy-in-judging-1",readMinutes:2,keypoint:"Certainty, spreading risk, and protecting a class of claimant are typical policy reasons, not private opinion.",h:"What courts count as a policy reason",body:[{t:"p",text:"A policy reason is not a judge's personal opinion about fairness. It is a claim about what an entire class of case needs."},{t:"list",items:["Certainty — will people be able to plan their affairs once this becomes the rule.","Spreading risk — can one side insure against the loss more cheaply than the other.","Protecting a class — does one side usually have less power to protect itself.","Floodgates — would a ruling for the bank let in many more claims like it."]},{t:"p",text:"In Caparo Industries plc v Dickman, a House of Lords case on auditor liability, the test for a duty of care has three parts."},{t:"p",text:"Foreseeability, proximity between the parties, and whether imposing a duty would be fair, just, and reasonable."},{t:"rule",text:"The third limb of the Caparo test for a duty of care asks directly whether imposing a duty would be fair, just, and reasonable. That is a policy question stated in the open, not a technical rule with a fixed trigger.",source:"Caparo Industries plc v Dickman [1990] 2 AC 605 (HL)"}]},{id:"sec-l-policy-in-judging-2",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"An apex court states policy more openly than a court bound tightly by precedent below it.",h:"How openly a court says so",body:[{t:"p",text:"Not every court reasons this openly. A court low in the hierarchy is bound by precedent and has less room to invoke policy directly."},{t:"p",text:"An apex court such as the Federal Court has more freedom, because it is not simply following someone else's answer."},{t:"caution",text:"A policy argument dressed up as if it were a rule is worse than an honest one. Say plainly that you are arguing about consequences, and defend the consequence you predict."},{t:"predict",prompt:"A new case asks whether a food delivery rider owes a duty of care to a pedestrian he nearly hits while speeding. Should the court's reasoning mention traffic on the roads generally?",options:["No — only the two people in this case matter to the court's reasoning","Yes — a rule here will govern every rider and pedestrian in a similar position","Only if the rider's employer asks the court to consider it"],answer:1,reveal:"Yes. A ruling here becomes a rule for every rider and every pedestrian afterward, so the court reasons about the class, not only these two people."}]},{id:"sec-l-policy-in-judging-3",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"Name the class affected, name the value at stake, and say why it beats the other side's value.",h:"Using a policy argument yourself",body:[{t:"p",text:"A policy argument you make must do three things, or it is just an opinion wearing a wig."},{t:"list",items:["Name the class of case affected, not only the two parties in front of the court.","Name the value at stake — certainty, protecting the weak, or spreading a cost fairly.","Say why that value should beat the value the other side is relying on."]},{t:"example",text:"For Yusof's bank: imposing a duty on valuers who know their reports will reach a lender protects lenders generally, at a cost valuers can insure against cheaply."},{t:"checkpoint",q:"Why is 'imposing a duty would be fair, just, and reasonable' a policy question rather than a rule?",options:["Because it has no fixed trigger and asks the court to weigh consequences","Because it appears in a House of Lords judgment","Because it only applies to auditors"],answer:0,why:"A rule fixes its trigger in advance. This asks the court to weigh what imposing the duty would do, which is a policy judgment, not a checklist."}]}],quiz:[]},{id:"l-floodgates",moduleId:"m13-legal-reasoning",title:"Hypotheticals and the floodgates argument",minutes:30,readMinutes:6,summary:"A floodgates argument predicts that a ruling will let in far more claims than the court can manage, and it can be right or wrong. The answer is never to deny more claims will follow — it is to show a boundary that keeps the class identifiable.",source:"Standard jurisprudential material on the floodgates argument in judicial reasoning, illustrated with Donoghue v Stevenson [1932] AC 562 (HL).",verify:"Nothing here states a rule of Malaysian law beyond the Donoghue citation. Check the current Malaysian test for a duty of care before relying on the neighbour principle directly, and read the judgment rather than this summary of the dissent.",lastVerified:"2026-09-11",reading:[{bookId:"schauer-thinking-like-a-lawyer",where:"on consequentialist arguments and the floodgates worry"},{bookId:"williams-learning-the-law",where:"on reading a dissent for what it feared, not only what it lost"}],sections:[{id:"sec-l-floodgates-0",readMinutes:2,keypoint:"Farid's own contract was with the shopkeeper, yet he wants to sue the manufacturer directly.",h:"A claim that seems to open a door",body:[{t:"p",text:"Encik Farid buys a sealed bottle of soy sauce from a sundry shop in Ipoh."},{t:"p",text:"He pours it over rice and finds a dead lizard near the bottom. He becomes ill for three days."},{t:"p",text:"Farid wants to sue the manufacturer directly. His contract of sale was with the shopkeeper, not with the manufacturer at all."},{t:"p",text:"The manufacturer's lawyer objects: allow this, and every buyer of every sealed product could sue every manufacturer directly, whatever went wrong."},{t:"rule",text:"A floodgates argument predicts that deciding a case one way will let in a flood of future claims the legal system cannot manage. It is an argument about consequences for the system, not about these two parties.",source:"Standard jurisprudential material on judicial reasoning"}]},{id:"sec-l-floodgates-1",readMinutes:2,keypoint:"A floodgates claim predicts how many future cases will follow, and predictions can be tested.",h:"What the argument actually claims",body:[{t:"p",text:"A floodgates argument is a prediction, not a fact. It says a ruling will cause many more claims of the same kind."},{t:"p",text:"The prediction can be tested. Ask how many people are actually in Farid's position, and whether they have any other reason not to sue."},{t:"p",text:"This is close to the objection raised against Mrs Donoghue's claim in 1932 — that a manufacturer should not answer to the whole world."},{t:"p",text:"In Donoghue v Stevenson, part of the dissent warned that recognising a general duty like this had no obvious stopping point."},{t:"rule",text:"The answer to a floodgates argument is not to deny that more claims will follow. It is to show a limiting principle exists that keeps the class of claimants bounded and identifiable.",source:"Standard common law reasoning on limiting principles in negligence, illustrated by Donoghue v Stevenson [1932] AC 562 (HL)"}]},{id:"sec-l-floodgates-2",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"Donoghue answered the floodgates fear with a limit, not by denying more claims would follow.",h:"Answering it: find the limit, or accept the flood",body:[{t:"p",text:"The majority in Donoghue v Stevenson did not deny that more claims would follow. They answered with a limit instead: a duty runs only to someone who could not inspect the product before using it."},{t:"p",text:"That limit — foreseeability and no chance to inspect — is what keeps the class of claimants bounded. It is not everyone who ever bought anything."},{t:"caution",text:"A floodgates argument used without naming any limiting principle is just an assertion dressed as prediction. Show the boundary, or the argument is only a fear."},{t:"predict",prompt:"A new claimant wants to sue a bridge's original builder fifty years after it opened, for a defect only now discovered. The builder warns of a flood of claims from every old structure in the country. Is that a strong floodgates argument?",options:["No, because the class can be bounded by how many structures actually have this defect","Yes, because age alone shows the class of claimants cannot be bounded","No, because builders are never liable for old work"],answer:0,reveal:"The second answer. The real question is how many structures share the actual defect, not how many structures exist in the country. That is a testable number, not a vague fear."}]},{id:"sec-l-floodgates-3",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"State a number and a boundary; without both, a floodgates claim is rhetoric rather than argument.",h:"Using the argument, and rebutting it",body:[{t:"p",text:"To use a floodgates argument well, name the boundary a ruling would need to cross. Say what class of case would be let in, and how large it is."},{t:"list",items:["Identify the class of future claimants the ruling would affect, not just this one.","Estimate whether that class is actually large, or only feels large.","Show whether an existing limit — proximity, foreseeability, a time limit — already bounds it."]},{t:"example",text:"Against Farid's claim: the class is not everyone who buys anything, only buyers of a sealed product who could not have inspected it. That class is already bounded by the same test Donoghue set."},{t:"checkpoint",q:"What actually defeats a floodgates argument?",options:["Showing that a limiting principle already bounds the class of claimants","Proof that the courts have enough time to hear the new cases","Pointing out that the argument is unpopular with claimants"],answer:0,why:"A floodgates fear is answered by a boundary, not by denying more claims exist. Foreseeability and proximity are the boundaries negligence actually uses."}]}],quiz:[]},{id:"l-obiter-dictum",moduleId:"m13-legal-reasoning",title:"Obiter dictum, and how much weight it carries",minutes:30,readMinutes:6,summary:"Obiter dictum is anything a judgment says beyond what the case in front of the court actually needed. It can still carry real weight, especially from a senior court reasoning carefully, even though it does not bind.",source:"Standard common law doctrine on obiter dictum and ratio decidendi, illustrated with Cavendish Square Holding BV v Talal El Makdessi [2015] UKSC 67.",verify:"Nothing here states a rule of Malaysian law beyond the Cavendish citation. Check the current Malaysian position on penalty clauses before relying on that case for anything beyond the reasoning technique.",lastVerified:"2026-09-11",reading:[{bookId:"mckinney-reading-like-a-lawyer",where:"on separating what a judgment decided from what it merely discussed"},{bookId:"williams-learning-the-law",where:"on ratio decidendi and obiter dictum"}],sections:[{id:"sec-l-obiter-dictum-0",readMinutes:2,keypoint:"The court only needs to answer one question here; anything beyond that is not needed to decide it.",h:"A remark that goes further than the case needs",body:[{t:"p",text:"Puan Nadia sells her interior design firm to Encik Boon for RM900,000, paid in two parts."},{t:"p",text:"She agrees not to compete for two years, or forfeit the last RM200,000 owed to her."},{t:"p",text:"She opens a rival studio after fourteen months. Boon refuses to pay the RM200,000, calling the clause a penalty the court will not enforce."},{t:"p",text:"A court deciding this dispute only needs to answer one question: is this particular clause, on these facts, a penalty. Anything it says beyond that is not needed to decide the case."},{t:"rule",text:"A statement in a judgment that goes beyond what was necessary to decide the case before the court is obiter dictum. It does not bind a later court the way the ratio decidendi does.",source:"Standard common law doctrine on obiter dictum"}]},{id:"sec-l-obiter-dictum-1",readMinutes:2,keypoint:"Obiter carries weight from the seniority of the court and the care the reasoning shows.",h:"Why obiter is not automatically worthless",body:[{t:"p",text:"Not binding does not mean unimportant. An apex court's careful, considered remark can shape the law even though no later court must follow it."},{t:"p",text:"In Cavendish Square Holding BV v Talal El Makdessi, the United Kingdom Supreme Court went far beyond the two contracts before it."},{t:"p",text:"Several justices set out, at length, how the whole doctrine on penalty clauses should be reframed for future cases."},{t:"p",text:"Much of that discussion went beyond the minimum needed to decide whether these two specific clauses were penalties. Later courts have leaned on it anyway."},{t:"rule",text:"The weight of an obiter remark depends on the seniority of the court, how carefully it was reasoned, and how directly it addresses the point later courts must decide. A careless aside carries little; a considered discussion by an apex court carries much more, even though it does not bind.",source:"Standard common law doctrine on obiter dictum, illustrated by Cavendish Square Holding BV v Talal El Makdessi [2015] UKSC 67"},{t:"predict",prompt:"The Federal Court, in deciding a straightforward contract dispute, adds three paragraphs speculating about how the same reasoning might apply to leases generally. Is that obiter?",options:["No, because the Federal Court's remarks always bind","Yes, and being from an apex court it may still carry real weight","Yes, and it therefore carries no weight at all"],answer:1,reveal:"Yes. It goes beyond the lease dispute at hand, so it does not bind. Coming from the Federal Court and reasoned with care, it may still guide later courts strongly."}]},{id:"sec-l-obiter-dictum-2",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"Calling a remark obiter says where it sits, not whether the court got it right.",h:"Obiter is not the same as wrong",body:[{t:"p",text:"Students sometimes treat 'obiter' as a way to dismiss a passage they find inconvenient. That is a mistake."},{t:"p",text:"Calling something obiter tells you it does not bind. It tells you nothing about whether the reasoning inside it was any good."},{t:"caution",text:"Do not use 'merely obiter' as a substitute for an argument. Say why the remark is unpersuasive, or accept that a later court may well adopt it anyway."},{t:"checkpoint",q:"A High Court judgment contains a considered discussion of a point not strictly needed to decide the case. What is the correct description?",options:["It is obiter, and its weight depends on how carefully it was reasoned","It is the ratio, because the judge clearly believed it","It is void, because only the Court of Appeal may reason this way"],answer:0,why:"A remark not needed to decide the case is obiter regardless of which court gave it. Its persuasive weight still depends on the reasoning and the court's seniority."}]},{id:"sec-l-obiter-dictum-3",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"Ask what the court had to decide, then test whether a passage was needed to decide it.",h:"Finding the line yourself",body:[{t:"p",text:"To find the line, ask one question of any passage: was this needed to decide the case in front of the court?"},{t:"list",items:["If removing the passage would change the order the court made, it is probably ratio.","If removing it changes nothing about the order, it is obiter, however confident the tone.","If several judges reason differently to the same result, compare them before you claim a single ratio."]},{t:"example",text:"In Nadia's dispute, the finding that RM200,000 was a genuine penalty is the ratio. A passing view on how forfeiture clauses in employment contracts should be treated is obiter."}]}],quiz:[]},{id:"l-principle-or-rule",moduleId:"m13-legal-reasoning",title:"Reasoning from principle versus reasoning from a rule",minutes:30,readMinutes:6,summary:"A rule fixes its trigger and applies once that trigger is met. A principle states the purpose behind the rule, and lets a court ask what that purpose requires on facts nobody wrote the rule for.",source:"General jurisprudential material on rules and principles in adjudication, illustrated with Donoghue v Stevenson and Caparo Industries plc v Dickman.",verify:"Nothing here states a rule of Malaysian law beyond the two citations used as illustration. Check the current Malaysian formulation of the duty of care test before relying on either case directly.",lastVerified:"2026-09-11",reading:[{bookId:"hart-concept-of-law",where:"on rules, their purposes, and the cases a rule's words do not clearly settle"},{bookId:"schauer-thinking-like-a-lawyer",where:"on reasoning from rules versus reasoning from the principles behind them"}],sections:[{id:"sec-l-principle-or-rule-0",readMinutes:2,keypoint:"Halim broke the curfew rule by twenty minutes, but he was fighting a fire at the time.",h:"The same words, two ways to read them",body:[{t:"p",text:"Encik Halim runs a vegetable stall in a wet market in Kuantan. Market rules say stallholders must leave the premises by ten at night."},{t:"p",text:"At 9:50pm, a fire breaks out in the wiring near the fish section."},{t:"p",text:"Halim stays until 10:20pm, helping two elderly stallholders out and dousing the flames with a hose before the fire brigade arrives."},{t:"p",text:"The market's management wants to end his licence. He broke the curfew rule, on its plain words, by twenty minutes."},{t:"rule",text:"A rule fixes a trigger and applies once that trigger is met, regardless of why. A principle states the purpose the rule serves, and a court reasoning from principle asks what that purpose requires on these facts.",source:"Standard jurisprudential material on rules and principles in adjudication"}]},{id:"sec-l-principle-or-rule-1",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"A rule-based answer checks only whether the trigger was met, nothing about why.",h:"Reasoning from the rule",body:[{t:"p",text:"Reasoning from the rule is mechanical, in the best sense. Was the stallholder present after ten? Yes. The rule is broken."},{t:"p",text:"This is predictable and cheap to apply, and it is why the rule was written as a fixed time rather than a vague standard."},{t:"p",text:"On this reading, Halim's reasons for staying do not matter. The clock is the whole test."},{t:"caution",text:"A purely mechanical reading can produce an answer nobody who wrote the rule would have wanted. That is the cost of choosing predictability over judgment."},{t:"predict",prompt:"Halim's licence review committee reads the curfew rule literally: present after ten means breach, full stop. Before you read on — is that reasoning from a rule or from a principle?",options:["From a principle, because it considers what the curfew was for","Neither — a committee cannot reason at all","From a rule, because it applies the trigger without asking why it exists"],answer:2,reveal:"From a rule. The trigger — presence after ten — is checked and applied, with no question asked about what the curfew is actually protecting."}]},{id:"sec-l-principle-or-rule-2",readMinutes:2,keypoint:"A principle asks what the rule is actually for, then measures conduct against that purpose.",h:"Reasoning from the principle behind it",body:[{t:"p",text:"The curfew rule exists to clear the market safely and keep it orderly overnight. That is the principle behind the words."},{t:"p",text:"Reasoning from the principle asks a different question: did Halim's presence after ten defeat that purpose, or serve it?"},{t:"p",text:"Answered this way, staying to fight a fire and evacuate people is not the mischief the curfew was written to stop."},{t:"p",text:"Courts reach for a principle like this most often when the existing rules run out — a genuinely new kind of case the rule-writer never had in mind."},{t:"example",text:"Before 1932, English law protected buyers of dangerous products only through a set of narrow, specific categories. In Donoghue v Stevenson, the House of Lords instead reasoned from a general principle: do not injure your neighbour."},{t:"rule",text:"The neighbour principle in Donoghue v Stevenson answered a case the existing narrow categories did not clearly reach, by asking what the underlying purpose of the law of negligence required.",source:"Donoghue v Stevenson [1932] AC 562 (HL)"}]},{id:"sec-l-principle-or-rule-3",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"Principle answers a wider range of cases but is more contestable and less predictable than a rule.",h:"Living with the choice",body:[{t:"p",text:"Reasoning from a broad principle is powerful exactly where rules run out, and risky for the same reason."},{t:"p",text:"A principle stated too widely decides cases nobody has thought through yet, with no earlier case to discipline the answer."},{t:"p",text:"Caparo Industries plc v Dickman pulled back from Donoghue's wide principle, deciding new duty-of-care cases incrementally, by comparing them to established categories instead."},{t:"p",text:"Two honest judges can disagree about a hard case for exactly this reason: one reasons from the rule's words, the other from what the rule is for."},{t:"checkpoint",q:"Why did Caparo move away from Donoghue's broad neighbour principle toward an incremental, category-by-category approach?",options:["Because the neighbour principle had been formally overruled by Parliament","Because auditors are outside the scope of the law of negligence","Because a broad principle can decide unforeseen cases with no earlier case to discipline the result"],answer:2,why:"A wide principle is powerful precisely because it reaches new situations, and that is also why it is harder to predict and easier to contest."}]}],quiz:[]},{id:"l-legal-reasoning",moduleId:"m13-legal-reasoning",title:"How legal reasoning is actually done",minutes:25,summary:"Rules and standards, reasoning by analogy, and why the answer is often genuinely open without being arbitrary.",plants:[],prepares:[],reading:[{bookId:"schauer-thinking-like-a-lawyer",where:"the whole book — rules, precedent, analogy and the realist challenge"},{bookId:"bingham-rule-of-law",where:"for what the constraint on judges is supposed to be for"}],source:"Schauer, Thinking Like a Lawyer; Hart on open texture; standard jurisprudential material.",lastVerified:"2026-09-07",verify:"Nothing here is a rule of Malaysian law and none of it needs a citator. The positions described are contested in the literature by design — read the book rather than treating this summary as the argument.",plate:"open-texture",plateCaption:"A settled core and a doubtful edge. The cases that reach a court come disproportionately from the ring, not the middle.",readMinutes:8,sections:[{id:"sec-l-legal-reasoning-0",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"Two competent lawyers read the same section and the same cases and disagree. Why that is not a scandal.",h:"Why competent lawyers disagree",body:[{t:"p",text:"Two lawyers read the same section and the same three cases, and they reach opposite conclusions. Neither of them is lying, and neither of them is incompetent. So how can that happen?"},{t:"p",text:"Everything in this course up to this point has taught you rules. This module asks a different question: what kind of thinking is legal reasoning, and why does it produce so much honest disagreement?"},{t:"p",text:"The answer is that the materials leave the outcome genuinely open far more often than a textbook's confident prose suggests. Knowing where that happens is what separates an argument from a recitation."}]},{id:"sec-l-legal-reasoning-1",readMinutes:2,keypoint:"Rules buy predictability and misfire on the odd case; standards fit every case and predict none.",h:"Rules and standards",body:[{t:"p",text:"Legal directives come in two shapes, and choosing between them is a real design decision with costs on both sides."},{t:"list",items:["A **rule** fixes its trigger in advance: drive no faster than 90 km/h. It is predictable and cheap to apply, and it will sometimes give a result nobody wanted — the ambulance, or the empty road at three in the morning.","A **standard** states the goal and leaves the application open: drive at a speed that is reasonable for the conditions. It fits every case, and tells you nothing at all until a court has ruled."]},{t:"rule",text:"Rules buy predictability at the cost of occasionally getting the individual case wrong. Standards buy accuracy in the individual case at the cost of predictability.",source:"Schauer, Thinking Like a Lawyer"},{t:"p",text:"Put simply: a rule is easy to obey and occasionally unfair. A standard is fair in principle and very hard to plan around."},{t:"p",text:"Noticing which of the two you are dealing with tells you what kind of argument is going to work."},{t:"p",text:"Against a rule you argue that the trigger is not satisfied, or that this is not a case the rule was ever meant to reach. Against a standard you argue the factors instead."},{t:"example",text:"Section 300(c) of the Penal Code is close to a rule: did you intend the injury, and would an injury like that normally kill? The reasonable person test in negligence is a standard rather than a rule. The argument that works against each of them is a different shape."},{t:"compare",title:"Two shapes, and the argument each one invites",left:{h:"Rule — the trigger is fixed in advance",items:["Predictable, and cheap to apply.","Occasionally gets the individual case wrong.","Easy to obey, and easy to plan around.","**To argue against it:** the trigger is not satisfied, or this is not a case the rule was ever meant to reach."]},right:{h:"Standard — the goal is stated, the application left open",items:["Accurate in the individual case.","Very hard to plan around in advance.","Fair in principle, and expensive to apply.","**To argue against it:** the facts do not meet the goal the standard states, or the considerations point the other way."]}}]},{id:"sec-l-legal-reasoning-2",readMinutes:2,keypoint:"Analogy is not mechanical: two cases are never identical, so the work is deciding which differences matter.",h:"Reasoning by analogy",body:[{t:"p",text:"Precedent works by analogy, and analogy is not a mechanical operation at all. Two cases are never quite identical to each other."},{t:"p",text:"So the question is always whether they are alike in the respects that matter. And what matters is decided by the rule you take the earlier case to stand for."},{t:"p",text:"That is why the argument about how generally a **ratio** is stated is not a technicality. The ratio decidendi is the rule a decision actually rests on, and it is the binding part."},{t:"predict",prompt:"A decided case holds that a landlord owed his tenant a duty to repair a broken stair. This year a visitor to the same house falls on that stair. Before you read on — does the earlier case govern it?",options:["Yes — the danger is identical, and so is the stair","It depends on how broadly the earlier case's rule is stated","No — that case was about tenants, and a visitor is not a tenant"],answer:1,reveal:"The middle one. The other two are the arguments each side is going to run, and both can be taken honestly out of the same judgment. Read the earlier case as a rule about landlords and tenants, and it does not reach the visitor. Read it as a rule about anyone in control of a dangerous stair, and it does. Nothing in the judgment settles which reading is the right one."},{t:"p",text:"State it narrowly and the earlier case can be distinguished away. State it broadly and the earlier case governs this one. Both statements can be defended honestly from the very same judgment."},{t:"example",text:"A case holds that a bus company owed a duty to a passenger hurt by a sudden stop. Read narrowly, it is a case about buses and their passengers. Read broadly, it is about anybody in charge of a vehicle and anybody riding in one. Which side wants which reading depends entirely on the next set of facts."},{t:"caution",text:"This is not a licence to say that precedent means whatever you want. The range of defensible statements is real but bounded, and an argument outside it will simply lose. The skill lies in knowing where those edges actually are."}]},{id:"sec-l-legal-reasoning-3",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"Words have a settled core and a doubtful edge, and litigated cases come from the edge.",h:"Words with fuzzy edges",body:[{t:"p",text:"Rules are made of words, and words have a settled core and an uncertain edge."},{t:"p",text:"Hart's example is a by-law prohibiting vehicles in a public park. A car is plainly caught by it, and a bicycle is arguable. A war memorial tank on a plinth is the sort of case that makes the point."},{t:"rule",text:"Legal language has a core of settled meaning and a penumbra of doubt. Most cases are decided in the core and never litigated; the cases you read are disproportionately from the penumbra.",source:"Hart, on the open texture of legal language"},{t:"p",text:"Put simply: most of the time everybody agrees what a word covers. The cases that reach a courtroom are the ones sitting out on the fuzzy edge."},{t:"p",text:"This explains something about your reading that is otherwise quite disorienting. The law reports contain the hard cases, because the easy ones settle or never start at all."},{t:"p",text:"So reading only reported cases gives a systematically distorted impression of how uncertain the law really is."},{t:"checkpoint",q:"Why do the cases in the law reports make the law look less settled than it really is?",options:["Because judges disagree with one another more than they used to","Because the cases that reach a court are the ones out on the fuzzy edge","Because the reports leave out the reasoning that made each case easy"],answer:1,why:"Most words have a settled core, and disputes in the core get obeyed, settled or never brought at all. What is litigated and reported comes from the penumbra. The trap is to treat your reading list as a fair sample of the law, and then conclude that everything is arguable. Most of it is not."}]},{id:"sec-l-legal-reasoning-4",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"Formalism and realism are worth being able to state, because most arguments reduce to one of them.",h:"Formalism and realism",body:[{t:"p",text:"Two positions are worth being able to state, because arguments about how judges decide usually reduce to a version of one or the other."},{t:"p",text:"**Formalism** holds that legal outcomes follow from the legal materials themselves. The rules, applied to the facts, give the answer, and the judge's job is to find it."},{t:"p",text:"**Realism** holds that outcomes are substantially driven by other things, and that the legal reasoning is a justification constructed afterwards."},{t:"p",text:"Neither of those extremes survives contact with practice. Most cases really are determined by the materials, but some really are not, and in those the judge is choosing."},{t:"p",text:"The useful question is not which of the two camps is right. It is which kind of case you are in, because that decides whether your best argument is doctrinal or consequential."},{t:"caution",text:"Do not deploy realism as a way of avoiding doctrine. An answer that says the judge will decide as they please is not a legal argument, and it is wrong about the great majority of cases."}]},{id:"sec-l-legal-reasoning-5",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"Take a case you have briefed and write its ratio twice, at two levels of generality.",h:"What to do with this",body:[{t:"p",text:"There are no flashcards or problems for this module yet. The exercise that pays here is to take a case you have already briefed and write its ratio twice."},{t:"p",text:"Write it once as narrowly as the judgment honestly allows, and once as broadly as it allows. Then say which side would argue for which version, and why. That is the whole module on a single page."}]}],quiz:[{id:"qlr1",q:"What is the trade-off between a rule and a standard?",options:["Rules are always better","Rules buy predictability at the cost of occasionally getting the case wrong; standards buy accuracy at the cost of predictability","Standards are always fairer","Rules come from statute and standards come from judges"],answer:1,why:"Which to prefer is a design question, and the answer depends on what the provision is for.",source:"Schauer, Thinking Like a Lawyer"},{id:"qlr2",q:"What is the 'penumbra of doubt'?",options:["A rule that has been overruled","The area where it is genuinely uncertain whether a word applies","A remark in a judgment that does not bind","An ambiguity that a definition section settles"],answer:1,why:"Most cases are decided in the core and never litigated. The ones you read come disproportionately from the edge.",source:"Hart on open texture"},{id:"qlr3",q:"What is wrong with using legal realism to say a judge will decide as they please?",options:["Realism has been discredited","It is not a legal argument, and it is wrong about how most cases are decided","It is true only of appellate courts","Judges are not allowed to consider policy"],answer:1,why:"The range of defensible positions is real, but it is bounded.",source:"Standard jurisprudential material"},{id:"qlr4",q:"What makes reasoning by analogy legitimate rather than arbitrary?",options:["The bounded range of defensible ways to state the earlier ratio","The judge's discretion","The agreement of the parties","The absence of a statute on the point"],answer:0,why:"You can state a ratio at more than one level of generality, but not at any level you like.",source:"Standard common law method on precedent and analogy"}]}];export{e as default};
